    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d362">

                <pb facs="0579" n="489" sameAs="frus:frus1932v02/pg489"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Executive Agreement Series No. 42</note>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">856.8561/8</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Netherlands Minister (Van
                            Royen) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 935</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-03-18">18 March, 1932</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Pursuant to instructions received from my
                    Government, I have the honor to enclose herewith copy of the Royal Decree of
                    January 29, 1932,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d362-f1"/> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Official Gazette</hi> No. 25) regarding load line regulations in the
                    Netherlands, purporting modification of the Royal Decree of September 22, 1909,
                    which was amended last by Royal Decree of November 4, 1926 and copy of which was
                    transmitted to Your Excellency by my note of January 20, 1931, No. 113.<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d362-f2"/></p>
                <p>According to this new Decree in certain cases a somewhat more lenient rule may be
                    adopted in the Netherlands with regard to load line marks, provided this will
                    not endanger ship and crew and will be in conformity with the minimum
                    requirements as stipulated in the International Load Line Convention of London
                    of July 5, 1930.</p>
                <p>I may remark at the same time that the Netherland Government, according to this
                    measure, has already put into force the stipulations of the London Convention
                    before it has been ratified.</p>
                <p>Please accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. H. van
                            Royen</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
